WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.760
An amputation is a major emergency that occurs when any part of the body is cut or torn off.

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:10.160
In this situation, your priorities are very clear.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:13.720
Focus on the injured person first, not the severed body part.

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:17.459
Only after any life-threatening bleeding is under control should you focus on preserving

00:00:17.459 --> 00:00:20.940
the body part for a doctor to potentially reattach later.

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:25.680
As soon as you recognize the injury, call 911 and send someone to grab your PPE and

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:27.160
a first aid kit.

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:30.320
Start by applying firm, direct pressure to the wound.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:34.880
If the bleeding is severe and won't stop with pressure alone, do not hesitate to apply

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:37.480
a tourniquet like we practiced earlier.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:42.000
Once the bleeding is under control, you need to act fast to protect the severed part.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:47.160
If possible, gently rinse it with clean water or sterile saline, then wrap it in moist sterile

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:49.619
gauze or a clean, damp towel.

00:00:49.619 --> 00:00:53.520
Place the wrapped part into a sealed plastic bag and write the patient's name and the

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.360
current time on the outside.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:00.680
To keep it cool, place that sealed bag into a second container or bag filled with ice.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:04.360
It is vital that you never place the body part directly on the ice.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:09.800
Doing so can cause severe tissue damage, making it impossible for surgeons to reattach it.

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:14.239
While you wait for EMS, remember to keep the patient warm and monitor them closely for

00:01:14.239 --> 00:01:15.199
signs of shock.

